    stem: 'A 22-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by ambulance following a motorcycle crash at 50 mph. On arrival, he is in severe respiratory distress, cyanotic, and gasping for air. Vital signs are: blood pressure 72/42 mmHg, heart rate 142/min, respiratory rate 38/min, and oxygen saturation 76% on a non-rebreather mask. Physical examination reveals trachea deviated markedly to the left, distended neck veins, and absent breath sounds with hyperresonance to percussion over the entire right hemithorax. Extensive subcutaneous crepitus is palpable across the right chest wall. The radiology technician is positioning the portable x-ray machine to obtain an upright chest radiograph. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?',
    choices: [
      ['Immediate needle decompression or finger thoracostomy in the right hemithorax without waiting for chest radiography', 'Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis characterized by hemodynamic collapse (severe hypotension, tachycardia), respiratory distress, unilateral absent breath sounds with hyperresonance, tracheal deviation AWAY from the affected side, and distended neck veins. It occurs when a one-way tissue valve allows air into the pleural space during inspiration but prevents egress during expiration, creating positive intrapleural pressure that collapses the ipsilateral lung and shifts the mediastinum, kinking the superior and inferior vena cava and abolishing venous return to the heart. Waiting for portable chest radiography in a hemodynamically unstable patient is a fatal error. Immediate decompression is required: needle decompression (using a 14-gauge catheter in the 2nd intercostal space midclavicular line or 4th/5th intercostal space anterior axillary line) or immediate finger thoracostomy, followed promptly by tube thoracostomy (chest tube).'],
      ['Wait 60 seconds for the portable chest radiograph to confirm pneumothorax before performing any invasive intervention', 'Waiting for imaging in tension pneumothorax causes cardiac arrest from complete cessation of venous return; clinical diagnosis mandates immediate intervention.'],
      ['Perform emergency endotracheal intubation with high-pressure positive pressure bag-valve-mask ventilation', 'Positive pressure ventilation in an undecompressed tension pneumothorax forces more air into the pleural space, accelerating cardiovascular collapse and death.'],
      ['Perform emergency bedside subxiphoid pericardiocentesis for suspected cardiac tamponade', 'Tracheal deviation and unilateral hyperresonance with absent breath sounds diagnose tension pneumothorax; pericardiocentesis does not treat a pleural air collection.'],
      ['Administer 2 liters of rapid intravenous crystalloids and a norepinephrine infusion to restore blood pressure first', 'Fluid resuscitation cannot overcome mechanical obstruction of the vena cava; decompressing the pleural space immediately restores cardiac output.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'Tension pneumothorax is a clinical emergency diagnosed by hypotension, unilateral absent breath sounds, hyperresonance, and contralateral tracheal deviation. Waiting for chest radiography is strictly contraindicated and can be fatal. Immediate pleural decompression (needle decompression or finger thoracostomy) must be performed immediately, followed by chest tube placement.',
    objective: 'Diagnose tension pneumothorax clinically and perform immediate needle or finger decompression without waiting for chest radiography.',
    takeaways: [
      'Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis: hypotension + absent breath sounds + hyperresonance + tracheal deviation.',
      'Never wait for a chest X-ray; immediate needle decompression or finger thoracostomy is life-saving.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'ATLS Advanced Trauma Life Support 10th ed. Chapter 4: Thoracic Trauma', url: 'https://www.facs.org/quality-programs/trauma/education/advanced-trauma-life-support/' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'Trauma + hypotension (72/42) + absent breath sounds + tracheal deviation = tension pneumothorax -> immediate needle decompression.',
    necessaryInference: 'Recognize that tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis requiring immediate needle decompression without waiting for X-ray.',
    strongestDistractor: 'Obtaining portable chest radiograph, a frequent exam trap that delays life-saving decompression in an unstable patient.',
    distractorExclusion: 'Any delay for imaging in a patient with BP 72/42 mmHg and tension pneumothorax leads to cardiac arrest.',
