    topic: 'Clostridioides difficile Infection & Fidaxomicin vs Oral Vancomycin',
    difficulty: 4,
    stem: 'A 68-year-old woman presents to the clinic with a 4-day history of watery diarrhea (6–8 bowel movements per day), low-grade fever, and lower abdominal cramping. She completed a 10-day course of oral ciprofloxacin for complicated diverticulitis 2 weeks ago. Physical examination reveals mild diffuse lower abdominal tenderness without guarding or rebound. Vital signs are: temperature 37.9 C, blood pressure 124/76 mmHg, and heart rate 84/min. Laboratory evaluation demonstrates: leukocytes 11,200/mcL (normal: 4,000–11,000/mcL) and serum creatinine 0.9 mg/dL (baseline: 0.8 mg/dL). Stool enzyme immunoassay is positive for Clostridioides difficile toxins A and B, and nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) is positive for the tcdB gene. This is the patient\'s first episode of C. difficile infection. According to current clinical practice guidelines, which of the following is the preferred first-line pharmacological treatment for this patient?',
    choices: [
      ['Oral fidaxomicin 200 mg twice daily for 10 days (or oral vancomycin 125 mg four times daily for 10 days)', 'Current clinical practice guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Society of America (IDSA) and Society for Healthcare Epidemiology of America (SHEA) recommend oral fidaxomicin (200 mg twice daily for 10 days) as the preferred first-line regimen for an initial episode of non-severe or severe Clostridioides difficile infection. Fidaxomicin is a macrocyclic bactericidal antibiotic that inhibits RNA polymerase with minimal systemic absorption and narrow-spectrum activity, preserving normal bowel microbiome (Bacteroides species) and significantly reducing recurrence rates compared to vancomycin. Oral vancomycin (125 mg four times daily for 10 days) is the established, acceptable alternative first-line agent. Oral metronidazole is no longer recommended as first-line therapy due to inferior cure rates.'],
      ['Oral metronidazole 500 mg three times daily for 10 days', 'Oral metronidazole was demoted in updated IDSA/SHEA guidelines and is no longer recommended as first-line therapy due to higher treatment failure and recurrence rates compared to fidaxomicin or vancomycin.'],
      ['Intravenous vancomycin 1 g every 12 hours for 10 days', 'Intravenous vancomycin is NOT excreted into the intestinal lumen in therapeutic concentrations and has ZERO efficacy against C. difficile colitis; vancomycin MUST be administered ORALLY.'],
      ['Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) administered via colonoscopy', 'Fecal microbiota transplantation is reserved for multiple recurrent episodes (at least 2 recurrences, meaning 3rd episode) failing antimicrobial therapy; it is not indicated for a first initial episode.'],
      ['Oral loperamide 4 mg initially followed by 2 mg after each loose stool', 'Antimotility agents (loperamide) are strictly contraindicated in active C. difficile colitis because slowing peristalsis traps toxins, precipitating toxic megacolon and intestinal perforation.'],
    ],
    explanation: 'Updated IDSA/SHEA guidelines establish oral fidaxomicin (200 mg BID for 10 days) as the preferred first-line treatment for an initial episode of C. difficile infection, with oral vancomycin (125 mg QID for 10 days) as the primary alternative. Oral metronidazole is no longer first-line due to inferior efficacy. IV vancomycin does not penetrate the colonic lumen. Loperamide is contraindicated.',
    objective: 'Identify oral fidaxomicin or oral vancomycin as the preferred first-line treatment for an initial episode of Clostridioides difficile infection, and recognize that oral metronidazole is no longer first-line.',
    takeaways: [
      'First-line for initial C. diff: oral fidaxomicin (preferred) or oral vancomycin.',
      'Oral metronidazole is no longer first-line due to inferior cure rates.',
      'Antimotility agents are contraindicated due to toxic megacolon risk.',
    ],
    references: [
      { label: 'Clinical Infectious Diseases 2021 IDSA/SHEA Focused Update: Management of Clostridioides difficile Infection', url: 'https://academic.oup.com/cid/article/73/5/e1029/6298219' },
    ],
    shortestValidSolution: 'Initial C. difficile colitis -> oral fidaxomicin (preferred) or oral vancomycin (acceptable alternative).',
    necessaryInference: 'Apply updated IDSA guideline preference for fidaxomicin/vancomycin and reject obsolete metronidazole.',
    strongestDistractor: 'Oral metronidazole, the historical board-exam answer that has been superseded by fidaxomicin/vancomycin.',
